 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed; the certificates node uses only this interface and never sees the storage backend.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Lookup is served from a local embedded SQLite index held in a file on the kiosk. The signed snapshot remains the unit of distribution and trust; the SQLite index is a derived artifact that each kiosk builds for itself from a snapshot it has already validated. Snapshot validation, index construction, and lookup all belong to this node.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. After validation succeeds, build a SQLite index for the snapshot in a temporary location — one row per record, keyed by the normalized serial number — and activate it by atomic rename only after the build completes. A failure at any step (signature, schema, uniqueness, or index build) leaves the previously active snapshot and its index in place and reports a useful error. A partially built index is never queryable.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup, then query the active SQLite index by the normalized serial. Serial-number punctuation remains significant and is preserved in the stored key. Return the active snapshot date in every result, including `NotFound`. A single equality lookup on the keyed column meets the 250 ms budget for 90,000 records within kiosk memory.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS index directory; managed search service | Keeps every lookup on files on the kiosk with no runtime network access, as the HLD requires; a single keyed-equality query is predictable and well inside the latency budget; there is no service to operate, and no credentials or external connectivity to hold. Each kiosk validates and builds its own index per snapshot. The networked alternatives centralize index maintenance and leave room for richer searches later, but they make lookups depend on the lab network — adding timeout, credential, and connectivity concerns to this node — and run against the no-runtime-network tenet. The certificate interface is identical under all three. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Index recovery outside of import: an index built on-kiosk can be lost or corrupted between snapshots (disk fault, interrupted write). Whether the kiosk rebuilds it from the retained snapshot automatically on next start, or treats the absence as an import-level failure to re-run, is unsettled.
